.TH "CONFFLE" 5 "September 2025" "atomdns" "atomdns authors"

.SH "NAME"
.PP
\fIconffile\fP \- configuration file for atomdns

.SH "DESCRIPTION"
.PP
A \fIconffile\fP specifies the internal servers atomdns should run and what handlers each of these
should chain. The syntax is as follows:

.PP
.RS

.nf
ZONE [ZONE]... {
    [HANDLER]...
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
The \fBZONE\fP defines for which DNS origins this server should be called, multiple zones are allowed and
should be \fIwhite space\fP separated.

.PP
When a query comes in, it is matched again all zones for all servers, the server with the longest match on the
query name will receive the query.

.PP
\fBHANDLER\fP defines the handlers(s) we want to load into this server. This is optional as well, but as
server with no handlers will just return SERVFAIL for all queries. Each handlers can have a number of
properties than can have arguments, see the documentation for each handler.

.PP
The order of the \fBHANDLER\fPs is the order in which they are executed! (If you know CoreDNS, this is
different, as which CoreDNS the order is fixed compile time).

.PP
Comments are allowed and begin with an unquoted hash \fB\fC#\fR and continue to the end of the line.
Comments may be started anywhere on a line.

.PP
Environment variables are supported and either the Unix or Windows form may be used: \fB\fC{$ENV_VAR_1}\fR
or \fB\fC{%ENV_VAR_2%}\fR.

.PP
You can use the \fB\fCimport\fR "handler" (See atomdns\-import(7)) to include parts of other files.

.SH "IMPORT"
.PP
You can use the \fB\fCimport\fR "handler" to include parts of other files, see atomdns\-import(7). To prevent infinite
recursion a maximum of a 1000 imports are allowed.

.SH "SNIPPETS"
.PP
If you want to reuse a snippet you can define one with and then use it with \fIimport\fP.

.PP
.RS

.nf
(mysnippet) {
    log
    whoami
}

example.org {
    import mysnippet
}

.fi
.RE

.SH "EXAMPLES"
.PP
The \fBZONE\fP is root zone \fB\fC.\fR, the \fBhandler\fP is \fIchaos\fP. The \fIchaos\fP plugin takes an (optional) argument:
\fB\fCatomdns-001\fR. This text is returned on a CH class query: \fB\fCdig CH TXT version.bind @localhost\fR.

.PP
.RS

.nf
\&. {
   chaos atomdns\-001
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
When defining a new zone, you either create a new server, or add it to an existing one. Here we
define one server that handles two zones; that potentially chain different handlers:

.PP
.RS

.nf
example.org {
    whoami
}
org {
    whoami
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
Is identical to:

.PP
.RS

.nf
example.org org {
    whoami
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
Reverse zones can be specified as domain names:

.PP
.RS

.nf
0.0.10.in\-addr.arpa {
    whoami
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
or by just using the CIDR notation:

.PP
.RS

.nf
10.0.0.0/24 {
    whoami
}

.fi
.RE

.PP
This also works on a non octet boundary:

.PP
.RS

.nf
10.0.0.0/27 {
    whoami
}

.fi
.RE

